infrastructure.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:28.380
I'm going to talk a little bit
about how we do that as well

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:31.650
as, how our team uses
Google Cloud to deliver

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:32.627
those services.

00:12:32.627 --> 00:12:34.710
And then I'll follow up
with some of the synergies

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.080
that we see when we
work with customers who

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.030
use Google Cloud themselves.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.550
First off, speaking
about Twistlock,

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:45.790
we provide, what we call
end-to-end container security.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:49.510
Because containers are portable
across any environment,

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.630
it's important to protect,
not only in production

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:56.350
but in staging, in registry,
even on developer workstations

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:58.700
depending on your environment.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.390
So we provide a
number of services.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.220
I'm going to highlight
three of them here,

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.590
vulnerability management,
runtime defense,

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:07.330
and compliance.

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:11.590
Our vulnerability management,
we scan containers

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:16.360
at any stage of life
cycle for malware, CVs,

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:18.100
misconfigurations.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:25.360
Any gaps that could cause holes
to open up in your environment.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:27.460
The way we do this
is we source a number

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:31.450
of feeds directly from vendors
and from threat providers,

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:32.920
threat intelligence themselves.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:35.560
We don't leverage NVD or
any aggregate clearing

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:36.910
houses of data.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.040
We go directly to the source.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.350
That's packages,
operating system,

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.150
all the layers of container.

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.940
We scan for vulnerabilities
in real time.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:48.250
We integrate with tools
like Jenkins or TeamCity

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.130
to alert or even
block builds based

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:55.880
on the number and severity
of vulnerabilities found.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.190
Our runtime defense
feature is really

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.440
what starts to separate
Twistlock apart,

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.340
and I really like to consider
is what transforms the way we

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.280
do security from the old world.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.730
At runtime, Twistlock
leverages the static analysis

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:14.970
that we've done in the
vulnerability piece.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.430
And it combines that
with machine learning

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.520
that analyzes the
contents of a container

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:24.150
and uses the contents and
the behavior of container

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:27.930
to create and automatically
enforce a security model--

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:31.880
no oversight required,
no manual model creation.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:33.380
That little bit of
machine learning

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:37.760
allows organizations to deploy
quickly and have security

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:39.770
for every container
in their environment

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.260
without having to write a
policy for every container

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:43.400
in their environment.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.950
When a new container comes up
and it's the same container

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.470
image, that policies
automatically transferred over,

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.180
automatically applied.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.610
This allows teams
to rapidly scale

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:57.920
what they do without having to
worry about how their security

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.200
organization keeps up with that.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.860
So that's the networks, that
syscalls, that's file system,

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.540
and that's the whole
processes running

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:06.950
in the container itself.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.920
It covers all four
layers of that.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:11.840
That's not where it ends though.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.810
We also offer
compliance to ensure

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.510
that your environment meets
either company or industry

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:21.770
standards from again,
[? build to ?] runtime.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:25.520
We work closely with
NIST to do things

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:27.080
like translate
the HIPAA security

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.600
rule into a
containerized environment

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.510
and provide HIPAA
compliance serve customers.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:35.270
We provide PCI compliance
to our customers.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:38.060
And we're fully extensible
with OpenSCAP standards.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:39.860
That means any
company that's looking

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:43.310
to provide secure compliance
across their environment

00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:46.600
can leverage Twistlock to do so.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:48.850
That's a little bit
about what we provide

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.820
as far as container security.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:53.440
And we provide all of this--
our development team leverages

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:55.260
Google Cloud to do so.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:58.130
So I'm going to talk
about that a bit.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.190
When we first started,
it was important for us

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:03.560
as a cloud native company,
as a company looking

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:08.300
to transform the way security
was done for the new world,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:10.580
we needed to find
a provider that

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:14.480
shared that same DNA as us, that
really understood that the way

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:19.660
software was being delivered is
transforming at a rapid pace.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:23.830
And it was so evident,
from the start even,

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:26.890
that Google Cloud was the
provider in the space that

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.350
had that shared
understanding, that shared

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.720
DNA, with Twistlock.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:33.380
I've got speed.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:34.190
I've got flexibility.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:35.398
I've got reliability up here.

00:16:35.398 --> 00:16:36.710
And I'll talk about those.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.390
But the thing that always
struck me coming in--

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.192
and I'm not a member of our R&amp;D
team who made this decision.

00:16:42.192 --> 00:16:43.650
But when we looked
at it, the thing

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.610
always struck me was
the shared terminology.

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.110
When you leverage
Google Cloud, when

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:50.840
you leverage Google Container
Engine, when you leverage

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:55.220
Google's Cloud services,
the way that the vocabulary

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.040
in the platform, in the console,
in the management tools,

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.170
is written and structured,
it's the same thing

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.419
you see in other
cloud native services.

00:17:03.419 --> 00:17:04.960
Google talks about
containers the way

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:06.329
Docker talks about containers.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:08.550
The way Kubernetes
talks about containers.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.800
You don't see that
with other providers.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:13.440
You see them trying to take
the language of old world

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.500
virtualization and
translate that over.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:18.720
And I know that seems
like such a small thing,

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.359
but when you're talking
about shared DNA, that

00:17:21.359 --> 00:17:23.220
was an immediate
first flag to us

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:25.140
that, hey, there's
some alignment

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:28.410
here let's keep going.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.840
When our R&amp;D team
evaluated, what we found

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.800
was Google Cloud Platform,
Google Container Engine, really

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:40.530
offered more speed than the
other options out there.

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:44.550
The Kubernetes native
integration, as it were,

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:48.690
allows our team to rapidly
deliver software and build

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.250
software on a
platform that's shared

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.590
by the majority of
our customers today.

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:57.030
The learns that we gain from
that process and our ability

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:00.720
to understand real world use
cases during the development

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:03.600
cycle, is extremely beneficial.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.790
Things like one
click upgrades allow

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:11.010
us to instantly patch or bring
up our entire environment

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.110
without worrying about,
did we get this part?

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:14.250
Did we get this part?

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:14.940
You click.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:15.730
It works.

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:17.900
Done.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:21.800
It's not just speed though,
it's the flexibility.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:28.430
The granular IAM controls,
the ease of user management,

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:32.810
really allows us to build
different environments

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:35.750
with different access for
different teams working

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.830
on different facets
of our product.

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:41.320
That type of granularity isn't
something you see in AWS.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:44.200
That's not something
you see in Azure.

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.610
That flexibility coupled with
a wide range of OS availability

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:53.340
for virtual machines really
was a compelling factor for us.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:56.190
The third piece, reliability.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.220
We're a security company.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:02.120
Our team needs to develop
as fast as threats come in.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:04.220
If there is a zero
day that we need

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.410
to update something in
our product against,

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:08.000
we need to have uptime.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.360
We can't have our VM's
down, our environment down.

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:14.090
Compared to Azure,
compared to AWS,

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:16.640
Google Cloud offered
significantly higher VM

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:20.280
availability and
uptime for our teams.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.369
The other piece, we're
a security company.

00:19:22.369 --> 00:19:24.660
We need to make sure that
the environment we develop in

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:26.770
is secure.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:32.370
So you look at Google Cloud,
you look at the way it notifies.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:35.700
When there's an
unpatched server, when

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.820
there's an incident, and there's
an open port on the firewall,

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:40.380
you get a notification.

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:41.670
That comes to you.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:43.800
There's no logging into
a centralized console

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.190
to get that information.

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:48.000
There's no digging
through a series of menus.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:51.420
That information is surfaced,
and it surfaced in real time.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.930
For a company that prides itself
on how we deliver security,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:58.747
seeing that in our cloud
provider was invaluable.

00:19:58.747 --> 00:20:01.080
Those are some of the reasons
that our team chose Google

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.150
Cloud for our deployments.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:06.480
It's helped us scale from
releasing a product a year ago,

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.310
to serving over 40
customers today.

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:11.860
I'd like to talk briefly
about one of those customers

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:14.020
and the scale and
the efficiencies

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.960
they're able to see by using
both Twistlock and Google

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:17.460
Cloud.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.840
Sadly, this is one of
those things where,

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.131
I'm a security company,
everyone seems a little jittery

00:20:24.131 --> 00:20:26.550
about being up on a
slide as oh, Twistlock

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:28.080
provides our security.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.700
Understandably so.

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:33.000
So we'll just say a leading US
media company, joint customer

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.250
of both Google
Cloud and Twistlock.

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:37.590
They have over 400
Docker hosts today

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:41.250
and plan to scale that number to
1,000 by the end of this year.

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:45.000
They have over 1,000
images in their registry.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.430
This spans 12 business
units, hundreds

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:52.530
of developers, all of which
make multiple deployments a day.

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:55.590
In the old world,
before Google Cloud,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.540
before Twistlock, to secure
this type of environment

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:03.800
would require teams.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:07.160
Teams for each
business unit here.

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:10.640
This is all done now with a
single security architect.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:14.870
A single person responsible
for monitoring and enforcing

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:18.350
policy across this
environment and ensuring

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:22.770
security and compliance
as this scale continues.

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:24.510
This is the cloud native dream.

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:28.170
This is the way the world
is moving where security

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:30.540
is no longer a stop sign.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:32.410
Security is no
longer a stoplight.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.130
It's a traffic camera.

00:21:34.130 --> 00:21:37.250
That's the promise of enterprise
security with DevOps agility.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:39.900
That's what we do
here at Twistlock.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.000
Feel free to come visit
us in the Startup Village

