00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:06.160
So before I started
Greta, I worked

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:09.080
at a large European
media company,

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:12.020
where we, amongst
other things, streamed

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:15.650
Champions League, which,
at least in Europe, is

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.570
a very big thing.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:20.000
But what we learned
the hard way,

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.130
really, was that
during the games,

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:25.640
and especially during
the most important ones,

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:30.260
our viewers would way too
often experience problems.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:34.130
On a bad day, it could
mean a crashed service.

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:37.280
But even on a good
day, they would often

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:41.360
be met by bad image
quality or buffering,

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:45.170
such as in this image.

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:48.140
In this room, I think,
we as consumers,

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:54.194
can all relate to how
extremely frustrating that is.

00:23:54.194 --> 00:23:56.820
It is exactly
situations like that,

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:00.120
that made us start
Greta, a completely

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:04.320
new way of delivering
content over the internet.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:08.310
We do this by creating a new
type of infrastructure layer

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.250
on top of traditional CDNs.

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:15.210
The reason is that we found
that despite using CDNs,

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.310
a lot of companies
really struggle

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:20.730
delivering that end user
experience that we really

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.780
think their users deserve,
and especially during the most

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:26.190
business critical times.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:32.670
Another example of
that is e-commerce.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.570
E-commerce companies often
spend months preparing

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:41.020
for new campaign launches or,
for example, Black Friday,

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:42.960
but when their
consumers stands there

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:46.790
with basically money in their
hand that they want to spend,

00:24:46.790 --> 00:24:51.690
they're often met by a crashed
site or an extremely slow one.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.710
And the reason for
that is basically

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:03.080
that the internet assets built
today isn't very scalable.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:06.320
And there are obviously
a million positive things

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.530
with internet, but it
has two major limitations

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:11.910
in the infrastructure today.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:15.020
And one of those
limitations is that asset

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:19.160
is built on physical hardware
networks such as, CDNs,

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:20.660
for example.

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:26.850
That capacity really is limited,
which means that obviously it

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.150
can't handle infinite load.

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:32.910
Another big limitation
is that it's

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:39.610
depending on the geographical
spread of the network.

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:44.460
And as you can see, that
isn't very evenly distributed

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:46.320
throughout the world.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:48.360
And we're currently
at the stage where

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.210
we're seeing consumers
in multimillion

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:55.830
cities such as Nairobi
and Mumbai, for example,

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:59.670
demanding the same type
of internet experiences

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:02.700
as we're used to here
in the US and Europe,

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:08.050
but without having the
infrastructure to support it.

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:10.600
So we're simply in a
situation where we have

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:13.840
a supply shortage of capacity.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.510
And that will only get worse.

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:21.440
We are more and more connected
people, more connected devices,

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:25.840
but we're also consuming
heavier media types

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:28.330
than we've ever done before.

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:32.950
We see that today in the form
of HD content, for example.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:39.230
But we're also seeing growing
trends such as VR and 4K.

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:43.980
Consumer services such
as, Twitch TV and Netflix,

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:48.290
for example, have also shifted
the way we consume content.

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:51.830
And it makes it more
likely, now than before,

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:55.190
that we're consuming content
at roughly the same time

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:58.250
of the day as for example,
our neighbors, which

00:26:58.250 --> 00:27:00.860
further increases the
load on the already

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:04.630
strained infrastructure.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:07.300
At Greta, we think
it's especially

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:10.030
those times when it's
extremely high load

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:13.840
and also often very business
critical such at Champions

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.520
League final or Black Friday.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:20.440
That's the situations that
we find most interesting.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:24.160
And the reason for that is
that by using our technology

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:27.400
you can actually provide the
best user experience when

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:29.530
it matters the most.

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:33.520
By adding more concurrent
users to your site or service,

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:36.940
you can actually deliver
higher quality faster

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:38.580
whilst offloading the servers.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:45.360
The reason that we're
able to achieve this

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:50.880
is that we utilize our own
decentralized data distribution

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:55.300
network as one of the ways
in which we deliver content.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.440
So basically, this means
that the more users you

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:02.160
add to your service or
site, the higher the density

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.380
in our network becomes.

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:08.430
Meaning that we can find more
optimal ways of delivering

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:09.645
content to your users.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:14.630
This is one of our users.

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:18.240
It's a global audio streaming
company using our technology

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:19.230
today.

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.210
And in the background,
you can basically

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:25.080
see a screenshots of how
their concurrent users

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:29.820
at one point in time are
forming a Greta network.

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:33.360
By using our technology, they've
increased their throughput

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:38.880
with average of three times,
whilst offloading more than 91%

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:40.080
to Greta's network.

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:47.560
One of the things that we
find challenging, but very

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:50.230
interesting with
building relatively

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:52.750
complex infrastructure
technology,

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.390
is packaging that
technology in a way

00:28:55.390 --> 00:29:00.440
that makes it truly accessible
to developers and companies.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:03.970
That's why we are very proud
that most of the sites using

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:08.380
our technology deploy our
script without any hands on help

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:09.400
from us.

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:11.740
And often, they have
it live in production

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:17.530
in less than 10 minutes,
which we find pretty amazing.

00:29:17.530 --> 00:29:20.140
By adding our script,
you get access

00:29:20.140 --> 00:29:23.210
to our analytics platform,
where we basically

00:29:23.210 --> 00:29:26.800
look at all your content
delivery solutions today

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.680
and how they affect your
performance and user

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:33.250
experience.

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:35.770
We then take all
that data and feed it

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:38.230
into our intelligent
routing algorithms

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:41.740
and can thereby determine
what route will actually

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:45.920
give the end user the
best possible experience.

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:48.880
Our product today consists,
amongst other things,

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:53.000
of CDN evaluation, peer-to-peer
delivery, Smart Cache

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:56.266
solutions, as well as
performance analytics

00:29:56.266 --> 00:29:56.765
and reports.

