00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:51.930
it's a web standard
where Google have been

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:55.360
one of the main initiators.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:59.080
Furthermore, at GCP, it's
obviously important for us

00:30:59.080 --> 00:31:01.810
to get access to the network.

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:05.400
And that is why we use
their load balancing.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:09.960
We use Google Container
Engine to host our services,

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:13.390
and we use BigQuery and
CloudSQL to store data.

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.790
We're obviously
continuously looking

00:31:19.790 --> 00:31:22.910
at how we can improve
what we do and how we

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:26.540
can do so with the help of GCP.

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:29.600
Some of the things that we're
really excited about adding

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:32.610
is the things to the right here.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:34.790
So for our persistent
data, we're

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:37.460
currently adding
Cloud Spanner, which

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:41.120
it has been a lot of talk
about here at GCP Next.

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:43.370
So basically, the
reason we use Spanner

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:47.960
is to be able to do data
replication with low latency.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:50.960
But the thing that we're truly
excited about and waiting for

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.720
is the global version
of Spanner, which will

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:57.300
be released later this year.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:00.180
In order to handle
our network data

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:05.320
we are adding Cloud Pub/Sub
together with Dataflow.

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:11.300
And this allows us to enrich
and analyze our network data.

00:32:11.300 --> 00:32:13.890
Furthermore, we
