00:34:34.635 --> 00:34:36.760
And you've got to remember
that they were at a time

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:38.176
when everyone was
saying that it's

00:34:38.176 --> 00:34:41.770
impossible to fly unless you
had a gas that was lighter

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:43.310
than air.

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:45.380
That was the common held belief.

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:46.880
And so there is a
common held belief

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:49.639
that's been around
for 25, 30 years,

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:51.830
that I want to share with
you today and then talk

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:56.130
a little bit about how
Incorta shatters that belief.

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:59.660
So at the beating heart
of every enterprise,

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:02.139
you will find an ERP system.

00:35:02.139 --> 00:35:04.430
You'll find something that's
registering every business

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:09.740
activity, every sale, every
order, every supply chain

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:12.685
event, any collections, revenue.

00:35:12.685 --> 00:35:14.810
They're all going to flow
through a data model that

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:16.350
looks something like this.

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:20.060
This data model is what we
refer to as a normalized model.

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:22.360
It's lots of tables with
lots of relationships.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:24.260
So you have an
online transaction

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:26.910
based processing system.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:28.310
This is actually
a schema, that's

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:33.140
a real world schema, that's
used significantly through most

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:36.440
of the Fortune 1000 companies
and this is actually

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:37.670
just one query.

00:35:37.670 --> 00:35:39.680
This is the content, these
are the relationships

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:42.560
in the joins that are needed
to give you something.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.110
Now, this model
works pretty well

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:49.430
when you're looking at
something like one transaction.

00:35:49.430 --> 00:35:52.670
This model falls apart
the moment you say,

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:55.520
I want to look at my
data across everything,

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.320
and I want to slice
and dice it any way I

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:01.740
want based upon the
information that I have.

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:05.660
And so for the last 25 years,
we've seen a lot of innovation.

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:07.600
But it's all been
down the wrong path

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:11.890
because they believed the joins
and relationships between data

00:36:11.890 --> 00:36:13.690
could never be fast.

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:17.830
Joins and sorts are what
kill a database performance.

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:20.230
So we started with extract
transform and load,

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:21.730
ETL processes.

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:23.230
People started to automate them.

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:25.180
Have automated testing for them.

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:28.150
All of this innovation
was poured into ETL.

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:31.180
People looked at star
schemas, or cube structures,

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:34.840
or summarized data, to be able
to put the data into a shape

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:37.990
that maybe the relational
database can minimize the joins

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:41.404
and give you that data
in a timely fashion.

00:36:41.404 --> 00:36:42.820
And then there was
other companies

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:44.195
that jumped up
and said, you know

00:36:44.195 --> 00:36:47.045
what, we're going to make really
hefty, big pieces of hardware.

00:36:47.045 --> 00:36:48.670
We're going to charge
a million dollars

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:51.830
plus for this piece of hardware
that is an appliance that

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:54.250
will sit in your data center
and promises to give you

00:36:54.250 --> 00:36:57.920
unparalleled performance.

00:36:57.920 --> 00:36:59.280
But what happened?

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.580
We had frustrated
business users and we

00:37:01.580 --> 00:37:04.050
had a super busy IT department.

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:07.410
And so people would spend
months, if not years,

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:09.570
iterating on star
schemas, trying

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:11.780
to find out what my
business requirements?

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:13.745
What do my business
users want to see?

00:37:13.745 --> 00:37:16.120
And then I'll spend the next
year and a half building it.

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:18.540
They'll take a look at it,
and then they would say, oh,

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:20.590
that's interesting.

00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:22.240
What about this?

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:23.830
Well, you didn't ask me that.

00:37:23.830 --> 00:37:25.820
Let me go back and
I'll come back.

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:29.430
And so they'll be looking
at data that is static.

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:32.490
Very, very much set
in stone in terms

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:35.190
of how they want to look at it,
but also it pretty much always

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:37.260
be yesterday's data.

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:42.290
These ETL processes can take 12
to 13 hours, nightly refreshes,

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:44.010
and people are just,
that's what we do.

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:45.218
That's what we've been doing?

00:37:45.218 --> 00:37:46.680
That's what we're expecting.

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.980
And you would think after
doing all this complex work

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:51.090
that it would be fast.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.170
Right?

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:54.630
Well, it was certainly faster
than a relational database

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:57.930
with joins, but
it was still slow,

00:37:57.930 --> 00:38:02.100
some jobs taking five minutes
even up to 90 minutes.

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:06.540
Run your job, go for lunch,
come back hoping that it's done.

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:08.470
And so Incorta steps
into that and said,

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:11.740
if we can do something
very special,

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:14.440
we can change the
fundamental approach

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:16.840
and reverse that decision
from 25 years ago,

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:18.850
and say, there is a better way.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:21.880
Incorta's Direct Data
Mapping is exactly that.

00:38:21.880 --> 00:38:24.490
You can think of it as a
technology that enables

00:38:24.490 --> 00:38:25.840
joins to perform.

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:28.840
It knows exactly how
every piece of data

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:32.720
within a relational model
relates to each other.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:34.130
No need for star schemas.

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:36.650
You can set this up
in hours and days.

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:37.850
You can refresh in minutes.

00:38:37.850 --> 00:38:40.250
We have customers
here in the Bay Area

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:42.977
that are upgrading
every five minutes.

00:38:42.977 --> 00:38:44.810
They're getting the
latest and greatest data

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:46.820
flowing into their system.

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:48.920
And with all of that
you say, OK, great.

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:49.730
It's faster.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:50.329
Looks easy.

00:38:50.329 --> 00:38:50.870
It's quicker.

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:53.690
Time to value, but
surely it's slower.

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:56.030
Well, it's not the
same speed for sure.

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:57.880
We're talking orders
of magnitude--

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:04.230
50, 100 times faster than some
of these other approaches.

